<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Organic Laws of Oregon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1843">1843</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Organic Laws of Oregon were the legal codes adopted by the Provisional Government of Oregon, first in July 1843 and revised in 1845. Based partly on the statutes of Iowa, they set up an executive, legislature and courts, banned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and allowed settlers to claim up to 640 acres of land. They governed the region until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oregon-territory">Oregon Territory</a> was organised in 1848.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/organic-laws-of-oregon.jpg" alt="Organic Laws of Oregon" width="320" height="251" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Organic_Laws_of_Oregon.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The laws grew out of meetings at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champoeg">Champoeg</a> on French Prairie in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/willamette-valley">Willamette Valley</a>. The first, in 1841, answered the death without a will of Ewing Young and appointed a probate judge, but went no further. A new series began on 2 February 1843 at the Oregon Institute, in what is now Salem, over predatory animals attacking livestock. At Champoeg on 2 May 1843 an assembly of fewer than 150 American and French-Canadian men voted article by article on forming a government. Accounts of the tally differ: William H. Gray gave 52 to 50, Robert Newell 55 to 50.</p><p>A Legislative Committee of nine, chaired by Robert Moore, first met on 15 May 1843, each member paid $1.25 a day. After speeches for and against its proposals at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champoeg">Champoeg</a> on 4 July, the laws were adopted by popular vote on 5 July 1843.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Modelled on Iowa's Organic Law and the Ordinance of 1787, the laws formed a temporary government to last until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> extended its jurisdiction. Their first section followed the Northwest Ordinance, except that wars against Native peoples were to be begun by representatives of the people, and banned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> except as punishment, without the Ordinance's clause on returning runaways. The vote went to every free male descendant of a white man, which let French-Canadians and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis</a> children take part. A three-member Executive Committee, the Legislative Committee and a judiciary made up the government.</p><p>The laws also set rules for marriage, divided the region into four districts and raised a militia of one battalion. A male settler could claim one tract and had to make permanent improvements within six months. Missions could claim six times as much, a provision resented by the American immigrants of 1843 and 1844 and repealed in 1844.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Scholars have judged the first laws very crude and unsatisfactory. In 1844 the legislature dissolved the Executive Committee in favour of a single governor, with an election set for 3 June 1845. The legislature of 1845, led by Jesse Applegate, held that changes needed the people's approval, and a vote on 26 July 1845 passed the amended laws, which replaced the Legislative Committee with a House of Representatives, at first of 13 members and allowed up to 61, which could change the laws by its own vote without referring them to the people.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Organic Laws of Oregon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The government's <b>Organic Laws</b> provided for a legislature, courts and land claims of up to 640 acres.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-oregon">Provisional Government of Oregon</a></li></ul></section>
